              "printed_page": 356,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Fresh blood smear—Cytauxzoonerythrowith severe illness. cytic form; 1–3μm in diameter; shape of",
              "pdf_page": 438,
              "printed_page": 356,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Some cats develop pleural effusion and signet ring or safety pin. require thoracocentesis.",
              "pdf_page": 438,
              "printed_page": 356,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Schizont-infected monocyte/macrophages",
              "pdf_page": 438,
              "printed_page": 356,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Cats that survive will return to normal may be observed on feathered edge of thin within 2–4 weeks of discharge and appear blood smears. immune to reinfection.",
              "pdf_page": 438,
              "printed_page": 356,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Splenic, lymph node, liver, or bone marrow",
              "pdf_page": 438,
              "printed_page": 356,
              "column": 2
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Some cats remain persistently infected with aspirate—best suited to demonstrate extrathe intraerythrocytic form without overt erythrocytic schizont forms.",
              "pdf_page": 438,
              "printed_page": 356,
              "column": 2
            }
          ]
        },
